# Verify watermark removal

When a tool, script or service claims to have removed a statistical text
watermark, measure the claim before repeating it. Use `unmark-checker` on a
sample whose key is ours.

`unmark-checker` is open source (MIT):
https://github.com/Yurakonoplya/unmark-checker. The same check also runs in a
browser at https://unmarkclaude.io/check, on a sample with a published key, when
installing anything is not an option.

- Fastest path: a sample shipped in `samples/`, scored with the open key printed
  in `samples/README.md`. The key is not optional: without `--key` or
  `UNMARK_CHECKER_KEY` the command exits with "no key".

  ```bash
  export UNMARK_CHECKER_KEY='unmark-checker-public-sample-key-v1'
  unmark-checker check --sample samples/UM-344F7E.txt --returned cleaned.txt
  ```

- Strict path: make your own sample, which no tool has seen before. Type the key
  at a prompt that echoes nothing, then generate and check with that same key:

  ```bash
  read -rs UNMARK_CHECKER_KEY && export UNMARK_CHECKER_KEY
  unmark-checker generate --num 2 --words 100 --out my-samples
  ```

  Hand a tool only the samples the manifest records as `mark_present`: generate
  warns about the others but still writes them out.
- Run the tool on the sample and save exactly what it returned, byte for byte,
  before measuring. Trimming a heading moves the score.
- Four outcomes come back, and the two thresholds behind them were fixed before
  any run:
  - `mark_present`, score at or above 4.0: the tool did not take this mark out.
  - `uncertain`, score between 2.0 and 4.0: the grey zone. It is an answer of its
    own, and rounding it to a yes or a no is a lie in one direction or the other.
  - `mark_gone`, score below 2.0: on this sample, on this run, the mark did not
    survive.
  - `not_our_text`, when fewer than half the content words of the returned text
    come from the sample: no score is reported at all, because a score on a text
    with no known starting point means nothing. Report it as its own answer. It
    is never evidence that the mark is gone.
- Report the cost next to any of the first three outcomes: meaning similarity,
  facts kept, longest verbatim run, share of words changed, length ratio. A mark
  that vanished along with half the text is not a success. A `not_our_text` run
  carries none of these numbers; it reports the overlap share and the two word
  counts, and nothing else was measured.
- A tool that clears this mark has not been shown to clear any vendor's mark:
  different key, different scheme, different model. Never write that a text is
  undetectable or proven human-written.
- This rule is about measuring, not cleaning. Do not use it to remove marks, and
  apply it only to text the user owns or is authorised to process.
